// conn_nil_field.cla (Task 4, binary-files phase): a `connection` FIELD
// (never assigned, so nil -- zero, like any other never-assigned int
// field) is a legal method-call receiver now (lowConnMethod lowers ANY
// receiver expression via lowExpr, lower.cla) but sending on it is a
// runtime contract violation, exactly like sending on a never-assigned
// local/global connection -- runtime/clarus/conn.cla's `h == 0` guard
// panics "use of nil connection" before ever touching rtConnState.
//
// `conn` below is otherwise unused -- it exists only so usesConn
// (check.cla, GLOBAL-var-only by design) is true and the host build
// splices conn.cla in at all; without it `rtConnSendText` (the runtime
// call r.c.send lowers to) would be an unresolved symbol, a link error
// instead of the intended runtime panic.
//
// Host-only pinning, same layout convention as conn_send_closed.cla
// (.cla + .err + .behavior). NOT yet exercised by T1 (behavior_test.go
// lives in internal/selfhost, which scripts/test-task.sh explicitly
// excludes) and NOT yet green under T2 either: TestBehaviorGoldens
// bootstraps from the COMMITTED clarusc/clarusc.c snapshot, which
// predates Task 4's whole "any receiver expression" lowering (the old
// lowConnMethod only ever resolved a bare global identifier) -- a
// record-FIELD receiver like `r.c` still hits the pre-Task-4 "method
// call on receiver kind 13 (not yet implemented)" diagnostic under that
// stale snapshot, so `clarusc emit` itself fails before this fixture
// ever runs. The .err/.behavior goldens below were computed against a
// CURRENT-source two-stage build (build-run/clarusc-current, the same
// bootstrap internal/conntest's buildConnFixture uses), matching this
// task's report; both will start passing once a later task regenerates
// clarusc.c from current source.
var conn: connection

record R { c: connection }

var r: R

on App.startCLI(args: list of string) {
    r.c.send("x")
}
